            <ArticleTitle>[Splenic injury as a complication of colonoscopy].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>A8006</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Splenic injury is a rare complication of colonoscopy. Due to the nonspecific clinical presentation of patients with a splenic injury, it is frequently overlooked.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE DESCRIPTION" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 75-year-old woman came to the emergency department with abdominal pain and fainting one day after undergoing colonoscopy. She was admitted to the hospital with the preliminary diagnosis of abdominal pain after colonoscopy. The following day a remarkable drop in her haemoglobin was seen. A CT scan with contrast showed a subcapsular bleeding in the spleen. The patient was treated conservatively and recovered well.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">In every patient with abdominal pain and fainting following colonoscopy, splenic injury should be considered in the differential diagnosis, especially in high-risk groups. If splenic injury is diagnosed in an early stage, adequate therapy can be started promptly.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
